    "q-19186": {
      "dom_id": "step-q19186",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6x-1}=$",
      "worked_solution": "We can calculate $\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6x-1}$ using synthetic division.\n\nFor synthetic division to work, we need the coefficient of $x$ in the denominator to be equal to $1.$ So first, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6x-1} & =\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6(x-\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process on $\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}$, we arrive at the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $18$ | $27$ | $0$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n|  | $18$ | $30$ | $5$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{(x-\\frac{1}{6})} & =\\frac{1}{6}(18x+30+\\frac{5}{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}) \\\\  & =3x+5+\\frac{5}{6x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6x-1}=3x+5+\\frac{5}{6x-1}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
